WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Start with the decision, then the margins.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.445
Write one paragraph that states the decision your benchmark informs.

3
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:17.861
For example: “Should our weekly meeting-notes pipeline (about 40,000 snippets a month) move from a frontier API model to a self-hosted fine-tuned model?

4
00:00:18.141 --> 00:00:30.344
” Everything else follows from that sentence: the volume sets the cost math, the pipeline sets the latency requirement (batch, so p95 matters little), and the users set which fields matter most.

5
00:00:30.624 --> 00:00:42.080
Then name the three systems precisely: the frontier model and its prompt, the base model you will fine-tune, and the fine-tuned model (base plus your adapter, served the same way as the base).

6
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:51.960
Name a fourth if the cost math calls for it: the cheapest API model you expect could pass, so the comparison isn’t only against the most expensive option.

7
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:01.115
Next, write the decision rule before any run: the metric (exact match on the frozen test set, with per-field accuracy reported alongside),

8
00:01:01.395 --> 00:01:13.619
the margin (“the fine-tuned model is acceptable if the lower bound of the paired 95 percent interval against the frontier model is above minus 3 points”), and the cost and latency conditions.

9
00:01:13.899 --> 00:01:20.619
Also write the scoring rules: what normalization each field gets, and that unparseable outputs count as wrong.

10
00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:27.747
These are the choices that are tempting to adjust after seeing results, which is exactly why they go in the plan first.

11
00:01:28.027 --> 00:01:35.984
Studio 3 called this deciding before you run; it is even more important here, because you built the candidate and you want it to win.
